Public-text guard (scripts/check_public_text.py)
================================================

NeoGraph is a public repository, so every tracked file is read by people who
never saw its history. The guard keeps wording and details out of that text that
only make sense inside the project: names of earlier projects, links to private
repositories, text that narrates what the owner or maintainer decided, text that
narrates a review panel, a roundtable or a vote of seats, home directories,
public IP addresses, key-shaped strings and private project names.

    python3 scripts/check_public_text.py --tree             # every tracked file
    python3 scripts/check_public_text.py --diff [BASE]      # lines added since BASE
    python3 scripts/check_public_text.py --list-rules

--diff defaults to origin/master and covers committed, staged, unstaged and
untracked additions, so it is the quick check to run before pushing. Each
finding is printed as "path:line: rule: excerpt" and the exit status is 1; a
secret-shaped excerpt is masked. Rules are listed by --list-rules and described
in the script's header. Predecessor, internal-tooling and private project names
are stored as SHA-256 hex digests of their lower-case form and matched by
tokenising scanned lines into words (and joined two-word or hyphenated forms),
lower-casing and hashing each token, so neither the guard nor its tests spell
out those names in plain text. Third-party and generated files (deps/,
THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES*, node_modules/, lock files, the interop spec copies under
bindings/python/tests/interop/*/spec/) and binary files are skipped.

CI runs it as the ctest entry public_text_guard (label: hygiene), next to
public_text_guard_selftest, which tests the script itself:

    ctest --test-dir build -L hygiene --output-on-failure

The scan covers the documents (Markdown, docs/, README*, CHANGELOG* and their
translations) as well as code, configuration and examples. --scope code skips the
documents and is for quick local runs only; neither CI nor the ctest entry uses it.

When a finding is a justified exception (a test fixture with a deliberately fake
key, a rule's own sample text, a shipped feature whose name is a flagged word, a
citation of published work), add a line to scripts/public_text_allowlist.txt:

    path-glob<TAB>rule<TAB>reason

Keep the entry as narrow as the thing it excuses and say why. Do not loosen a
rule to let a fixture through. To run the --diff check before every commit,
call it from .git/hooks/pre-commit.
